Well, I can two paths that life-as-we-know-it can follow:  One is that 
something (new ice age, large meteor impact, atomic/biological warfare, 
peak oil) causes computers and the Internet to become irrelevant to 
and/or too expensive for human survival.  After a decade or so, most 
digital media will be gone, so (assuming a later reconstruction) all 
languages would be starting on a common footing.  And depending on the 
catastrophe, either non-English speaking regions could recover faster, 
giving their languages an advantage, or English could splinter into many 
sub-languages, just as Latin splintered into the Romance languages.  In 
those cases, surviving copies of "NetHack for Dummies" may as well be 
written in Sanskrit or Old English.

The other path is that machine intellegence eventually exceeds that of 
humans and the Singularity arrives.  At that point, any language can be 
cheaply translated into any other, and anyone can use software written 
in any language.  Within a generation, infants get bablefish inserted in 
their ears at birth and everyone grows up learning something similar to 
Lojban.


